{"name":"Hitchcock Milk","hex":"#eeffa9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"milk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A white liquid produced by the mammary glands of female mammals to nourish their young. From certain animals, especially cows, it is also called dairy milk and is a common food for humans as a beverage or used to produce various dairy products such as butter, cheese, and yogurt.","A white (or whitish) liquid obtained from a vegetable source such as almonds, coconuts, oats, rice, or soy beans."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To express milk from (a mammal, especially a cow).","To draw (milk) from the breasts or udder."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/milk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alfred Hitchcock","blocks":[{"text":"Sir Alfred Joseph Hitchcock was an English filmmaker. He is widely regarded as one of the greatest and most influential figures in the history of cinema. In a career spanning six decades, he directed over 50 feature films, many of which are still widely watched and studied today. Known as the \"Master of Suspense\", Hitchcock became as well known as any of his actors thanks to his many interviews, his cameo appearances often near the beginning of his films, and his hosting and producing the television anthology Alfred Hitchcock Presents (1955–65). Among other accolades, his films garnered 46 Academy Award nominations, including six wins, although he never won the award for Best Director, despite five nominations."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Hitchcock%2C_Alfred_02.jpg/330px-Hitchcock%2C_Alfred_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred_Hitchcock","wikidata":"Q7374"},{"title":"Milk","blocks":[{"text":"Milk is usually a white liquid food produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of nutrition for young mammals before they are able to digest solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including calcium and protein, as well as lactose and saturated fat; the enzyme lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity. The first milk, which is called colostrum, contains antibodies and immune-modulating components that strengthen the immune system against many diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk","wikidata":"Q8495"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg/500px-Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg/1280px-Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg/500px-Site_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_%28Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Petrol station at the site of 517 High Road, Leytonstone, where Hitchcock was born; commemorative mural at nos. 527–533 (right)[16]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASite_of_517_High_Road_Leytonstone_London_E11_3EE_(Birthplace_of_Alfred_Hitchcock).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Number_13.jpg/500px-Number_13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Number_13.jpg/500px-Number_13.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hitchcock (right) during the making of Number 13 in London","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANumber_13.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Hitchcock_sculpture%2C_London%2C_2007.jpg/500px-Hitchcock_sculpture%2C_London%2C_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Hitchcock_sculpture%2C_London%2C_2007.jpg/500px-Hitchcock_sculpture%2C_London%2C_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hitchcock sculpture at the site of Gainsborough Pictures, Poole Street, Hoxton, north London[54]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHitchcock_sculpture%2C_London%2C_2007.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg/500px-Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg/1280px-Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg/500px-Hitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hitchcock and Reville on their wedding day, Brompton Oratory, 2 December 1926","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHitch_Gets_Hitched.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/The_39_Steps_Still.jpg/330px-The_39_Steps_Still.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.